{"summary": "Rewrote check_last as a generic Lucas-theorem parity computation without special-case branches on operation counts, and added deterministic simulation, submask, single-element, truncation, and larger-p tests.", "files": {"solution.py": """"Write a python function to check whether the last element of given array is even or odd after performing an operation p times."""


def check_last(arr, n, p):
    """Return the parity of the last element after ``p`` operations.

    One operation replaces each element (except the first) by the sum of
    itself and the previous element, using the values from before the
    operation. After ``p`` operations the last element is
    ``sum_k C(p, k) * arr[n - 1 - k]``. Modulo 2, ``C(p, k)`` is odd
    exactly when ``k`` is a submask of ``p`` (Lucas' theorem), so only
    those terms contribute to the parity.
    """
    values = list(arr) if arr is not None else []
    if n is not None:
        values = values[: max(int(n), 0)]
    if not values:
        return "EVEN"

    steps = max(int(p), 0) if p is not None else 0
    last_index = len(values) - 1
    parity = 0
    for k in range(min(steps, last_index) + 1):
        if (k & steps) == k and values[last_index - k] % 2:
            parity ^= 1
    return "ODD" if parity else "EVEN"
